PIE kʷeys-, “to see, observe, heed” → Old Latin coira → Classical cūra — “care, concern, administration.” Not derived from tending or nurturing; derived from seeing. The verb cūrāre built on it, then the agent noun cūrātor, then English curator.

The line in the doc that matters most: “authority constituted by observation, not asserted independent of it.” Care, in this etymology, isn’t a feeling that precedes attention — it’s what attention becomes once it’s sustained long enough to earn responsibility for what it’s looking at. That’s the same claim as the plane: you don’t get to declare care any more than you get to declare a landing place; you only get to have actually watched.

